## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Legend</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of the main plot and characters in short stories. Reading: Extract key facts and details from informational texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by reading base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of the main plot and characters of simple legends (e.g., "The hero was fighting the monster when the village was attacked."). Can extract key facts from texts about Panamanian legends (e.g., "The legend of La India Dormida takes place in El Valle de Antón."). Can read words with past tense (-ed) /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/ and recognize their pronunciation (e.g., emphasis goes in the pronunciation of /ɪd/ in "waited" in the sentence, "The villagers waited for the hero.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of famous Panamanian legendary characters or settings (e.g., a monster, a hero, a witch, a village, a river, a mountain). Students work in pairs to recall and name the legends or characters they discussed in Lesson 1, using target vocabulary like 'legend', 'hero', 'monster', 'witch', 'village', 'story'. They share their ideas aloud with the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board related to a legend, e.g., "Long ago, a brave hero saved the village from an evil monster." The teacher reads the sentence aloud, modeling clear pronunciation, especially of the past simple verbs 'saved' and '-ed' endings. The teacher highlights the adverbs of time 'long ago'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks concept checking questions to ensure understanding of key terms and concepts related to legends and past events.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a hero a good character or a bad character?" (Expected answer: Good character.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do legends usually happen now or long ago?" (Expected answer: Long ago.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides a short, simplified narrative excerpt of a well-known Panamanian legend (e.g., "La India Dormida"). The text is approximately 100-120 words, focusing on the main plot, characters, and using past simple and past continuous structures, as well as target vocabulary like 'legend', 'mountain', 'princess', 'warrior', 'love', 'fight', 'disappear', 'transform'. (Genre: Narrative, Register: Informal, Format: Short story text).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Telling a story: "Once upon a time, the hero saved the village." Sequencing events: "First, the hero saw the witch. Then, he fought with her."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the legend for the first time. The teacher asks: "What is the name of this legend?" and "Who are the main people in this story?" (e.g., "The princess and the warriors").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements related to the main events or characters in the text (e.g., "The princess was happy when the warriors were fighting." - False). Students read the text again to check their answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match characters from the legend to their main actions or descriptions (e.g., Princess - 'loved a warrior', Warrior - 'fought bravely').</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of main plot and characters. Extracting key facts. Grammar: Past continuous (e.g., was fighting), Past simple (e.g., celebrated, ended). Vocabulary: legend, monster, hero, village, fight, save, rescue, brave, strong, mysterious. Pronunciation: Practice pronunciation of inflectional endings -ed (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Cloze activity):</b> Students receive a worksheet with the legend text again, but with several past simple or past continuous verbs (e.g., 'fought', 'was waiting', 'celebrated') missing. They choose the correct verb form from a box to complete the sentences, focusing on grammatical accuracy and context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Phonemic Awareness focus):</b> The teacher writes a list of verbs from the legend ending in '-ed' (e.g., 'waited', 'fought', 'attacked', 'loved', 'ended', 'explored'). The teacher models the pronunciation of the '-ed' endings (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/). Students then read the words and categorize them into three columns based on the sound of the '-ed' ending. The teacher provides feedback and correction. (e.g., 'waited' goes under /ɪd/, 'attacked' under /t/, 'loved' under /d/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair interaction (Question & Answer Reading):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading specific sentences from the legend aloud to each other, emphasizing the correct pronunciation of '-ed' verbs. Afterwards, they ask and answer 2-3 "Wh-" questions (e.g., "Who was fighting?", "Where did the story take place?") about the legend, checking their comprehension against the text.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> 'Legend Fact File' Creation and Reading Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a different short text about another Panamanian legend (or a different, extended version of "La India Dormida" for variety, or a different segment). Their task is to read the legend carefully and extract key facts and details, identifying the main plot, characters, setting, and 2-3 significant events. They then create a "Legend Fact File" on a large sheet of paper or whiteboard, summarizing these facts using bullet points, simple sentences, and a relevant drawing. Finally, each group chooses one member to 'present' their fact file by reading the summary points aloud to another group or the class, clearly articulating the information they extracted.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> "My Favorite Legend". Students consolidate their reading comprehension skills by focusing on specific elements of a legend and presenting these facts. The texts provided should incorporate grammar points (Past simple, Past continuous) and vocabulary (e.g., 'hero', 'creature', 'treasure', 'explore', 'venture', 'bravely', 'mysteriously').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension, extracting key information, reading aloud for clarity, use of target vocabulary and grammar, and collaborative interaction in clarifying details.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the 'Legend Fact File' presentation, and a short individual comprehension quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Presentation:</b> Teacher observes groups as they present their "Legend Fact File," noting their ability to read and convey extracted information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Individual Quiz:</b> Students complete a short, individual multiple-choice or matching quiz based on the legend read in Stage 2, assessing their comprehension of the main plot, characters, and key events.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (for both presentation and quiz):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows comprehension of the main plot and characters (e.g., identifies main idea, protagonists/antagonists).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Extracts key facts and details from the informational text (e.g., 3-4 factual points about the legend).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reads words with past tense (-ed) /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/ intelligibly during presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., 'hero', 'monster', 'village', 'fight', 'brave') and simple past/past continuous structures effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in presenting extracted facts (for presentation).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I understood well about the legend today is: ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase I practiced reading today is: ______."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about Panamanian legends was most interesting or challenging for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying the '-ed' sounds help you read the legend more clearly?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the students demonstrate comprehension of the main plot and characters of the legends? Were they able to extract key facts effectively from the texts?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the reading material appropriately leveled for A2.2? What specific vocabulary or grammatical structures caused difficulty?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the phonemic awareness activities effective in improving pronunciation of '-ed' endings? What adjustments could improve this?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific scaffolds (e.g., pre-teaching vocabulary, graphic organizers, peer support) were most beneficial for supporting reading comprehension?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading comprehension and extracting facts from legends. For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the knowledge and vocabulary gained today to actively discuss and narrate short versions of these legends to their peers, practicing fluency and interactive communication using the past simple and past continuous.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>